  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA mid/upper ridge continues to build into the Central Plains while a \nvigorous mid/upper trough was moving into Southern California. This \nridge will remain in place through Saturday resulting in a very slow \nprogression of the west coast trough. This will keep the higher \nprobabilities for rain west of the our forecast area through \nSaturday while above average temperatures prevail. We could see\nsome highs around 80 today and Friday, with no record highs\nanticipated. Record highs for today and Friday range from \n88-93 across the area.\n\nAs the lee trough deepens on Friday, the increasing pressure \ngradient will allow for breezy southerly winds, highest over central \nand especially western KS. Considerable mid and high level clouds \nwill also increase as we move through the period, however above \naverage temperatures are expected to prevail with highs mostly in \nthe mid and upper 70s late in the week and into the weekend. As\nthe lead wave lifts over the Northern Rockies/Northern Plains \non Sat, the sfc cold front may be forced further south impacting\nparts of central KS which would impact forecast highs along the\nI-70 corridor. If this trend persists, we could see an earlier\narrival of cooler air on Sat afternoon for some areas.\n\nIncreasing chances for showers with embedded thunderstorms may \narrive late Sunday into Monday as the mid/upper trough finally \nemerges from the Southern Rockies. A cooler and more seasonable \nairmass will also arrive on Monday-Tuesday with highs struggling to \nclimb out of the 50s in parts of central KS. Moderating temperatures \nand dry weather will quickly return as we move into the middle of \nthe week.",
